    category: "business",
    faq: [{"question":"How is commission calculated?","answer":"Multiply sales by rate divided by 100 to get pay. Total equals sales plus commission. For example, $9,600 at 7% gives $672 and $10,272 total. Enter amounts in this tool to see splits update live. No signup is needed. See /terms."},{"question":"Are calculations local?","answer":"Yes. Deal value and rate math runs locally in your browser. For example, a $14,200 deal never uploads, works offline free, and clears when you close the tab. No login and no server log — sums clear when you close the tab. Your sales facts stay private on your device. See /terms."},{"question":"Can I handle tiered or split commissions?","answer":"This tool does flat-rate math plus manual slab runs. For example, $13,000 at 5% gives $650; run higher slabs apart and add totals. For teams, split the $1,140 payout by share, like 60/40. This is math help only. Confirm plans with sales ops. See /terms."},{"question":"What distinguishes flat versus tiered commission structures?","answer":"Flat applies one percentage across entire revenue, while tiered escalates rates after thresholds to reward overachievement. For instance, $18,000 at flat 6% yields $1,080, whereas 5% to $12,000 plus 8% beyond yields $600 plus $480, also $1,080 but incentivizing stretch goals differently across quarters."},{"question":"How should managers document splits to avoid disputes?","answer":"Document each participant share, slab thresholds, payout dates, and clawback conditions in signed sheets before quarter begins. For example, record 60/40 division of $7,500 yielding $4,500 and $3,000, plus 5% versus 8% tiers, then archive PDFs with payroll for transparent audits and timely reconciliation."}],
    howTo: [{"name":"Enter sales amount","text":"Type total deal value like $9,600 or $14,200 into sales field, using gross amount before refunds, discounts, taxes."},{"name":"Enter commission rate","text":"Type commission percent like 7% or 7.5% into rate box; for example 10% on $2,000 equals $200 payout instantly."},{"name":"View results","text":"See commission like $672 on $9,600 at 7% plus $10,272 total update live; copy figures for payroll records."},{"name":"Compare scenarios","text":"Try 5% versus 10% on $10,000 to contrast $500 against $1,000, then test tiered slabs to negotiate splits confidently."}],
    guide: [{ heading: "Flat commission math", body: "Commission equals sales multiplied by rate divided by one hundred, while total compensation adds sales plus commission for payroll clarity. For example, $9,600 at seven percent yields $672 commission and $10,272 combined, whereas $14,200 at eight point five percent produces $1,207 commission and $15,407 total, both recalculated instantly as you type. Enter gross deal value excluding refunds, chargebacks, and value-added taxes to keep figures comparable across representatives and quarters. Use consistent currency formatting, preserve two decimal places for cents, and copy results into paysheets promptly. Edge case: negative returns require separate credit memos rather than negative sales entries, preventing distorted percentages. This calculator handles arithmetic only, leaving tax withholding and employment classification to payroll systems. Test three scenarios monthly to calibrate quotas, identify outliers, and align incentives with revenue targets effectively." }, { heading: "Tiered slab rates", body: "Tiered structures pay escalating percentages once revenue crosses predefined thresholds, motivating sellers to exceed quotas rather than settling near minimums. For example, five percent applies to the first $12,000, then eight percent covers amounts above that line, so an $18,000 agreement generates $600 from the base plus $480 from the $6,000 excess, totaling $1,080 commission. Run each slab separately in this tool and sum outcomes for transparent verification across pay periods. The progression below illustrates incentives: | Slab | Applicable revenue | Rate | Commission | First $12,000 | $0-$12,000 | 5% | $600 | Excess $6,000 | $12,001-$18,000 | 8% | $480 |. Edge case: mid-cycle threshold adjustments demand prorated calculations rather than retroactive application, avoiding overpayment disputes. Save slab printouts, timestamp approvals, and share written schedules before quarter kickoff." }, { heading: "Team splits and limits", body: "Team distributions divide a single commission pool according to agreed ownership percentages before disbursement, preventing payroll confusion during collaborative closes. For example, a $7,500 pool split sixty forty awards $4,500 to the lead and $3,000 to support, while $13,000 at five percent creates $650 shared proportionally across two contributors in separate runs. Enter each member allocation as an independent calculation, label beneficiaries clearly, and export figures for accounting reconciliation. Limitation: this utility performs flat and manual slab arithmetic solely, excluding income tax, refunds, chargebacks, currency conversion, and legal enforceability for complex enterprise agreements. Confirm tier definitions with sales operations beforehand. For margin analysis after payouts, continue at /profit-margin-calculator to assess profitability. Archive signed sheets quarterly, audit variances promptly, and communicate adjustments transparently to sustain trust." }],
