    guide: [{ heading: "What the ATS checker does", body: "An applicant tracking checker quantifies overlap between resume diction and posting requirements, surfacing matched competencies and absent phrases in a unified dashboard. Paste a two-page resume containing 400 words alongside a 300-word advertisement to receive instant diagnostics. For example, forty extracted terms with twenty-eight hits produce seventy percent coverage plus twelve gaps including Docker and Jest, guiding targeted revisions. Recruiters routinely filter two hundred submissions to twenty human reads using similar lexical screens, so alignment influences visibility substantially. Include standard headings, month-year tenure, and eight to ten core skills to maximize parseability across Workday, Greenhouse, Lever, and iCIMS. Edge case: image-based PDFs conceal characters, therefore paste plain narrative rather than uploading scans. This analysis evaluates vocabulary solely, ignoring layout elegance, credential validity, and interpersonal fit." }, { heading: "How keyword matching works", body: "Matching extracts salient nouns, verbs, frameworks, and certifications from postings, discards common stop words, then checks literal presence within resume prose. For example, lifting sixty-four percent to eighty-eight percent requires inserting eight absent skills across two revision cycles, prioritizing exact titles like Next.js and CI/CD because fuzzy variants evade detection. Maintain natural phrasing while mirroring terminology to satisfy both algorithms and hiring managers reviewing top-ranked output. The reference below clarifies impact: | Signal | Parser success | Guidance | Single-column standard headings | High | Recommended | Two-column text boxes | Low | Avoid | Scanned image PDF | Minimal | Never use |. Edge case: acronyms with slashes or periods fragment unpredictably, so replicate punctuation precisely from source listings. Re-run after each edit, track delta improvements, and preserve quantified verbs demonstrating scope, scale, and business outcomes." }, { heading: "ATS worked example: 400 words to 72 percent match", body: "Paste a 400-word professional summary plus a 300-word vacancy to observe seventy-two percent coverage with twelve deficiencies flagged for remediation. Integrate Docker and Jest naturally within achievement bullets, quantify deployments and test coverage, then re-evaluate to approach ninety percent across two iterations. Limits: lexical auditing ignores typography, color, graphics, factual accuracy, and interview performance, therefore cannot guarantee shortlisting despite optimized density. It supplements rather than replaces strategic networking, portfolio evidence, and tailored outreach. For polished single-column formatting that parses reliably, construct original documents at /resume-builder before scoring iterations here. Retain plain fonts, left alignment, and chronological clarity to support both machine ingestion and rapid recruiter skimming. Validate dates meticulously, eliminate duplicate tooling mentions, and align action verbs with posting lexicon consistently." }]
